Z||000003||ChadMan||04-20-2001||12:56 PM||||Hard to argue against Ohio...<P>Most Ohioans are incapable of driving at a steady speed - any steady speed.  They come to a full stop before merging with an expressway.  They will not relinquish the passing lane to faster traffic.  They are afraid to exceed 45 mph in the rain, 25 in the snow...even if the snow melts when it hits the pavement.  They don't watch the traffic light and therefore miss it turning green.  They are either too fearful to turn right on red, or they turn right on red without checking for oncoming traffic.<P>Hoosiers are just as bad though, if not worse (sorry, Boilermaker).  How Indianapolis can be home to the world's most famous speedway AND have freeways with a 50 mph speed limit (downtown Indy) is beyond me.<P>Best drivers:  Southerners (except Atlanta).  They're either fast, courteous, or both.||||reg||12
Z||000004||FieroLisa||04-20-2001||01:12 PM|||| seems to me that North Carolina drivers do not know how to merge or drive in any bit of freezing rain :-)||||reg||1
Z||000005||AkursedX||04-20-2001||01:41 PM||||Well I can remember a while ago in a readers digest I had, they had a rating of the 5 worst cities to drive in.  <P>It went something like <BR>1 Boston<BR>2 Atlanta<BR>3 Los Angeles<BR>4 ummm...I forgot<BR>5  NYC<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||04-20-2001||02:00 PM||||My brother and his wife went to Manhatten, several years ago. Drove everywhere. Said that they had no trouble at all, due to all the training they had gotten, driving in Atlanta.<P>I cast my vote for Miami, though. Was there for two weeks, and by the time I got back to Atlanta, people were looking at <I>me</I> like <I>I</I> was crazy.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
