Z||000008||Jaygee79||04-20-2001||02:38 PM||||For a state, Rhode Island (sorry Phil  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>For a city, Boston.  You haven't seen anything until you've driven through Boston.<P>Providence should be second.<P>------------------<BR>Jaime<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pooheey1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||TheFiero64||04-20-2001||03:06 PM||||.<B>IMHO</B><P>In MA the rule is stay clear of RI drivers. I usually will see someone driving like an idiot and notice that it has RI plates.<P>As for Boston and rest of NE. We have very aggressive drivers but, probably some of the best in the nation. Just because we always have experience in varying weather conditions. You hardly ever hear of 5-10 car pile ups in New England.  <P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Old Lar||04-20-2001||03:35 PM||||I'd have to say that the drivers around Boston are the worst.  I remember driving down in the right (slow lane)on Rt 95 at 80 mph and being passed in the breakdown lane by several drivers only to have them swerve in and jam on their brakes as traffic came to a halt.<P>Chicago is another place to stay clear of, driving 70-80 mph to keep up with traffic in a posted 55 mph zone, then jam on the brakes because of traffic conjestion.  Maybe 294 around Chicago is a better way to get around Chicago..toll booth every 10 miles...Mayor Daily still lives.||||reg||3
Z||000011||rogergarrison||04-20-2001||04:38 PM||||Id say ohio myself. they dont know what merge or yield mean and no clue whatsoever what 'lane ends ahead' means. I get out of town to lonely country roads to get away from everyone and relax.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||LarryB||04-20-2001||07:54 PM||||If the best you can do is 80 on Rte 128 (only newbies and tourists call it I-95) your car belongs in the breakdown lane.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Seriously, if they passed a cop who wasn't  in a hurry to Dunkin' Donuts just the breakdown lane escapade would cost them $100 and points on their insurance.<P>The only thing that compares to a Boston driver is a cabbie in Manhattan. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>I'd have to say that the drivers around Boston are the worst.  I remember driving down in the right (slow lane)on Rt 95 at 80 mph and being passed in the breakdown lane by several drivers only to have them swerve in and jam on their brakes as traffic came to a halt.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
